<h3>5th String Major Chords Ascending</h3>
<p>Play these chords ascending then descending.</p>
<p><img class=3D"alignnone size-full wp-image-1239" src=3D"https://www.guit=
arlessonworld.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/12/5thstringchordexercise.gif" al=
t=3D"Major Chords Rooted on the 5th String" width=3D"900" height=3D"238"></=
p>
<h3>Other Ascending and Descending Exercises</h3>
<p>I=E2=80=99ll leave these ones for you to figure out, which is a great me=
ntal exercise in itself.</p>
<ul>
<li>You=E2=80=99ll notice that I used the major chords in the E and A form.=
 &nbsp;Try playing the diatonic chord sequence in the Key of C: CMaj, Dm, E=
m, FMaj, GMaj, Am, Bdim. For example, replace EMaj with Em.&nbsp; Although =
diatonic harmony is a topic of its own, I am defining it for these exercise=
s&nbsp;as the chords that use the notes of the major scale. &nbsp;In this c=
ase the C major scale.</li>
<li>Change the chords to the diatonic seventh chords in the Key of C from t=
he E and A forms: CM7, Dm7, Em7, FM7, G7, Am7, Bm7b5.</li>
<li>Because chords are constructed on thirds (major and minor third interva=
ls), try playing in thirds (every other note on a string) so you know the n=
otes of a chord along a string.&nbsp; This works for all diatonic chords in=
 a key.&nbsp; Let=E2=80=99s take E minor 7 for an example:&nbsp; E-(skip F)=
-G-(skip A)-B-(skip C)-D.&nbsp; The Em7 notes are E G B D.&nbsp; If you wan=
t Em, skip the last note.&nbsp; I love this exercise.&nbsp; It teaches so m=
any nuances of music at once.</li>
</ul>
<h2>More Exercises</h2>
<p><a href=3D"https://gum.co/secretsoflearningthefretboard" data-gumroad-si=
ngle-product=3D"true" target=3D"_blank"><img class=3D"size-medium wp-image-=
1227 alignright" src=3D"https://www.guitarlessonworld.com/wp-content/upload=
s/2015/12/TheSecretsofLearningTheFretboard-3D-215x300.png" alt=3D"The Secre=
ts of Learning the Fretboard Book" width=3D"215" height=3D"300" sizes=3D"(m=
ax-width: 215px) 100vw, 215px"></a>Here are just a few additional exercises=
 that you can use.</p>
<ul>
<li>Play a note then play its octave on the same string (12 frets up). &nbs=
p;Repeat this for all the notes.</li>
<li>Play a note then play the same note on an adjacent string. Example. A o=
n the 5th string, open; A on the 6th string, 5th fret.</li>
<li>Play a note then play its octave on an adjacent string. Example E on th=
e 6th string, open; E on the 5th string, 7th fret.</li>
<li>Play a note then play its octave two strings away. Example: E on the 6t=
h string, open; E on the 4th string, 2nd fret. &nbsp;Notice how these octav=
es are used in&nbsp;the&nbsp;chord forms that you know.</li>
<li>Play a chord and identify the root notes in that chord.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote><p>There are a lot more exercises, which is why I=E2=80=99m off=
ering&nbsp;a book to organize it all so you can focus on learning the fretb=
oard quickly! It&nbsp;contains 159 exercises to keep your practice sessions=
 fresh. &nbsp;It incorporates music theory, chords, and much more so you=E2=
=80=99re learning multiple concepts at once. &nbsp;I&nbsp;still use these e=
xercises to stay fresh. &nbsp;They work for beginners to pros. &nbsp;Consid=
er <a href=3D"https://gum.co/secretsoflearningthefretboard" data-gumroad-si=
ngle-product=3D"true" target=3D"_blank">buying my book, <em>The Secrets of =
Learning the Fretboard</em></a>!</p></blockquote>
<h2>Final Thoughts</h2>
<p>I hope you enjoyed this lesson on Learning the fretboard. &nbsp;It takes=
 a lot of practice, but staying motivated seems to be the #1 problem. &nbsp=
;There are a lot of fun ways to practice the guitar. &nbsp;Keeping your pra=
ctice fresh with varied exercises is the best way. &nbsp;Once you accomplis=
h it, you=E2=80=99ll break out of ruts and have a lot more fun with the gui=
tar!</p>
<p>If this lesson helped you, please like <a href=3D"https://www.facebook.c=
om/guitarlessonworld">my brand new facebook page</a> and consider posting a=
bout how it helped you.</p>
